        "text": "I get confused on the difference between propagater networks and dataflow.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1769970073.454439",
        "parent_user_id": "U08HU0GT52A",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "dI1aO",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "I get confused on the difference between propagater networks and dataflow."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "user": "U08HU0GT52A",
        "type": "message",
        "ts": "1770117159.559499",
        "client_msg_id": "ed51bc9b-6e09-4fdc-939a-f31af18fc0d7",
        "text": "A propagator network is different from a usual dataflow network mainly in\nwhat lives at the nodes.\nIn a propagator network (in the sense of Gerald Jay Sussman\u2019s work),\nnodes are autonomous cells that accumulate partial information, rather\nthan variables that are assigned single values.\nDependencies are implicit in the propagation structure, and computation\nproceeds by information growth until a fixed point is reached.",
        "team": "T5TCAFTA9",
        "thread_ts": "1769970073.454439",
        "parent_user_id": "U08HU0GT52A",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "rich_text",
                "block_id": "L+Kcg",
                "elements": [
                    {
                        "type": "rich_text_section",
                        "elements": [
                            {
                                "type": "text",
                                "text": "A propagator network is different from a usual dataflow network mainly in\nwhat lives at the nodes.\nIn a propagator network (in the sense of Gerald Jay Sussman\u2019s work),\nnodes are autonomous cells that accumulate partial information, rather\nthan variables that are assigned single values.\nDependencies are implicit in the propagation structure, and computation\nproceeds by information growth until a fixed point is reached."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
